= Solution
{parent=a}

The crude incidence-rate estimator is
$$
\widehat q_{SE}=\frac{10}{1000\times3}=\frac1{300}
$$
per person-month. This treats all 3000 person-months as susceptible time and assumes infection times are observed exactly, follow-up is complete, and each first infection is detected. In practice tests occur intermittently, so infection is <interval censoring>[interval-censored]; latent, brief, or asymptomatic infections may also be missed.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $P(t)=e^{Qt}$ be the <transition probability matrix> of the four-state <continuous-time Markov chain>, and put $N=\{S,R\}$ for a negative test and $P=\{E,I\}$ for a positive test. Starting susceptible at time zero, the likelihood contribution is
$$
\sum_{a\in N}\sum_{b\in P}\sum_{c\in N}
P_{Sa}(1)P_{ab}(1)P_{bc}(1).
$$
Equivalently, with indicator diagonal matrices $D_N,D_P$ and the susceptible basis vector $e_S$ it is
$$
e_S^TP(1)D_NP(1)D_PP(1)D_N\mathbf1.
$$
This sums over every hidden state sequence compatible with the three test results.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For transition-intensity vector $q$ and generator $Q(q)$, the expected infectious occupancy over three months from $S$ is the <Markov reward model> quantity
$$
m(q)=\mathbb E_S\int_0^3\mathbf1_{\{X_t=I\}}dt
=\int_0^3[e^{Q(q)t}]_{SI}\,dt.
$$
Insert the fitted intensities to obtain $m(\widehat q)$, evaluating the matrix exponential and integral numerically if necessary.

If $\eta=(\log q_{SE},\log q_{EI},\log q_{IR})$ has estimated covariance $V$, calculate the numerical gradient $g=\nabla_\eta m(e^\eta)$ at $\widehat\eta$. The <delta method> gives estimated variance $g^TVg$ and the approximate confidence interval
$$
m(\widehat q)\pm z_{1-\alpha/2}\sqrt{g^TVg}.
$$
Simulation from $N(\widehat\eta,V)$ followed by transformation through $m$ gives a useful asymmetric alternative.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The linear <SEIR model> with a constant susceptible-to-exposed intensity is
$$
\dot S=-q_{SE}S,
\qquad
\dot E=q_{SE}S-q_{EI}E,
\qquad
\dot I=q_{EI}E-q_{IR}I,
\qquad
\dot R=q_{IR}I.
$$
Adding the four equations confirms <conservation of population>: $S+E+I+R=N$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The separable equation $\dot S=-q_{SE}S$ with $S(0)=S_0$ has solution
$$
S(t)=S_0e^{-q_{SE}t}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Substitute part (ii) into $\dot E+q_{EI}E=q_{SE}S_0e^{-q_{SE}t}$. Multiplication by the <integrating factor> $e^{q_{EI}t}$ gives
$$
\frac d{dt}\bigl(e^{q_{EI}t}E(t)\bigr)
=q_{SE}S_0e^{(q_{EI}-q_{SE})t}.
$$
Since $E(0)=0$, integration and rearrangement yield
$$
E(t)=\frac{S_0q_{SE}}{q_{EI}-q_{SE}}
\left(e^{-q_{SE}t}-e^{-q_{EI}t}\right).
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= iv
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

At an interior maximum, $\dot E(t)=0$, equivalently
$$
q_{EI}e^{-q_{EI}t}=q_{SE}e^{-q_{SE}t}.
$$
Thus
$$
t_{\max}=\frac{\log(q_{EI}/q_{SE})}{q_{EI}-q_{SE}}.
$$
This is positive for distinct positive rates. Its continuous limit when $q_{EI}=q_{SE}=q$ is $1/q$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= e
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The $S\to E$ transition must be redefined. A constant $q_{SE}$ gives each susceptible the same external infection pressure even when no infectious person remains. For influenza, the <force of infection> should depend on the current infectious prevalence. The progression $E\to I$ and recovery $I\to R$ can retain rates $q_{EI}$ and $q_{IR}$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= ii
{parent=e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

With frequency-dependent transmission parameter $\beta$, the nonlinear SEIR equations are
$$
\dot S=-\beta\frac{SI}{N},
\qquad
\dot E=\beta\frac{SI}{N}-q_{EI}E,
\qquad
\dot I=q_{EI}E-q_{IR}I,
\qquad
\dot R=q_{IR}I.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= f
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

While $I>0$, divide the susceptible equation by the recovered equation:
$$
\frac{dS}{dR}=-\frac{\beta}{Nq_{IR}}S.
$$
Taking $R(0)=0$ and integrating gives
$$
S(t)=S_0\exp\!\left[-\frac{\beta}{Nq_{IR}}R(t)\right].
$$
At the end of the epidemic $E_\infty=I_\infty=0$, so conservation gives $R_\infty=N-S_\infty$. Defining
$$
\mathcal R=\frac{\beta}{Nq_{IR}},
$$
we obtain the <final size relation for an epidemic>
$$
S_\infty=S_0\exp[-\mathcal R(N-S_\infty)].
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

