= Solution
{parent=a}

If $U\sim\operatorname{Uniform}(0,1)$, the <inverse transform sampling> formula
$$
T=-\frac1\lambda\log U
$$
has survival function $\mathbb P(T>t)=e^{-\lambda t}$ and hence the required <exponential distribution>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Without censoring, every $v_i=1$ and $\mathbb E T_i=1/\lambda$. Therefore
$$
\frac{\mathbb E\sum_i v_i}{\mathbb E\sum_i x_i}
=\frac n{n/\lambda}=\lambda.
$$
This is events divided by total <person-time at risk>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The first two mechanisms are standard independent, noninformative censoring. The third relabels the event time itself and therefore is not independent censoring for the original latent event time; it is more naturally interpreted as assigning a cause in a <competing risks model>. The detailed calculations follow in the three subparts.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Fixed administrative censoring $C=\tau$ is noninformative because it is unrelated to the random event time. The censored fraction is
$$
q=\mathbb P(T>\tau)=e^{-\lambda\tau},
\qquad
\tau=-\frac{\log q}{\lambda}.
$$
Moreover, $\mathbb E v=1-q$ and
$$
\mathbb E X=\int_0^\tau e^{-\lambda t}dt=\frac{1-q}{\lambda},
$$
so the event-to-time ratio remains $\lambda$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For an independent $C\sim\operatorname{Exponential}(\mu)$, censoring is noninformative and
$$
q=\mathbb P(C<T)=\frac\mu{\lambda+\mu},
\qquad
\mu=\frac{q\lambda}{1-q}.
$$
The observed time is exponential with rate $\lambda+\mu$, while $\mathbb E v=\lambda/(\lambda+\mu)=1-q$. Thus
$$
\frac{\mathbb E v}{\mathbb E X}
=\frac{\lambda/(\lambda+\mu)}{1/(\lambda+\mu)}=\lambda.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Independent relabelling gives $\pi=q$, $\mathbb E v=1-q$, and leaves $X=T$ with mean $1/\lambda$. Its ratio is therefore $\lambda(1-q)$ rather than $\lambda$. To retain event hazard $\lambda$, simulate the common occurrence time with rate
$$
\lambda_{\mathrm{all}}=\frac\lambda{1-q}
$$
and label it as the target event with probability $1-q$. This is equivalent to independent competing clocks with target-event rate $\lambda$ and censoring-cause rate $q\lambda/(1-q)$; their minimum has rate $\lambda/(1-q)$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= 5
