= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Cox proportional-hazards model> is
$$
h_i(t)=h_0(t)e^{\beta z_i},
$$
where $h_0$ is an unspecified baseline hazard and $z_i$ indicates treatment. With no tied events, the <Cox partial likelihood> is
$$
L(\beta)=\prod_{i:\,v_i=1}
\frac{e^{\beta z_i}}{\sum_{j\in R(x_i)}e^{\beta z_j}}.
$$
Equality of the two hazard functions is $H_0:\beta=0$, testable with a likelihood-ratio, score, or Wald test from this likelihood.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use each matched pair as a separate <stratum> with its own baseline hazard and fit one common treatment coefficient $\beta$. The <stratified Cox model> partial likelihood multiplies the within-pair risk-set contributions, eliminating all pair-specific baselines. Test $H_0:\beta=0$ by the corresponding conditional score, likelihood-ratio, or Wald test.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A pair is informative only when its first observed exit is an event while both members remain at risk; later events after one member has left contribute a factor one. The uninformative pairs are therefore pair 5, with tied events at $(17,17)$, and pairs 10, 11, and 13, whose earlier observations are censorings: $(63+,278)$, $(89+,1083+)$, and $(141,138+)$. At $(140,140+)$ both are in the risk set immediately before time 140, so pair 12 is informative.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Among the nine informative pairs, the active patient fails first once and the placebo patient fails first eight times. The conditional partial likelihood is proportional to
$$
\left(\frac{e^\beta}{1+e^\beta}\right)^1
\left(\frac1{1+e^\beta}\right)^8,
$$
so
$$
e^{\widehat\beta}=\frac18.
$$
Under $H_0$, the number of active-first failures is $\operatorname{Binomial}(9,1/2)$. The exact two-sided <sign test> has
$$
p=2\mathbb P\{\operatorname{Binomial}(9,1/2)\leq1\}
=\frac{20}{512}\approx0.0391,
$$
so the data reject at the 5% level and favor lower hazard under active treatment.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Matching controls strong prognostic differences between patients and can greatly improve precision by making treatment comparisons within homogeneous pairs. It also protects against confounding when treatment assignment is not perfectly randomized globally.

Its disadvantage is loss of information: pairs with censoring before a comparison or tied outcomes contribute little or nothing, and the analysis cannot estimate the effects of pair-level matching variables. Poor matches and the logistics of paired enrollment can also reduce efficiency relative to a well-adjusted unpaired analysis.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= 6
{parent=Paper 207}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

With constant hazard $\theta$, observation $i$ contributes
$$
\theta^{v_i}e^{-\theta x_i}.
$$
Writing $D=\sum_i v_i$ and $T=\sum_i x_i$, the <log-likelihood> is $D\log\theta-\theta T$, whose derivative is $D/\theta-T$. Hence
$$
\widehat\theta=\frac{\sum_i v_i}{\sum_i x_i},
$$
the number of events divided by total person-time.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

<Left truncation> means that an individual enters observation only after a delayed entry time $L_i$ and is included only if the event time exceeds $L_i$. Earlier failures are absent from the dataset, so analysis must condition on survival to entry.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

In the <Kaplan–Meier estimator>, replace the ordinary risk set at event time $t$ by
$$
R(t)=\{i:L_i<t\leq X_i\}.
$$
The product-limit factor remains $1-d_j/|R(t_j)|$, but only individuals who have entered and not yet exited contribute to its denominator.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Conditioning on survival to $L_i$ changes the constant-hazard likelihood contribution to
$$
\theta^{v_i}e^{-\theta(x_i-L_i)}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\widehat\theta=\frac{\sum_i v_i}{\sum_i(x_i-L_i)},
$$
using only person-time accumulated after entry.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For period survival analysis over a calendar window, patients diagnosed earlier enter at the window's start only if still alive. Their survival times are left-truncated at the elapsed duration from diagnosis to that date, while follow-up is censored at the window's end. This estimates survival using mortality experience in the chosen recent period without pretending that earlier survivors were under observation from diagnosis.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

There are seven deaths by day 28. Their total time plus 28 days from each of the ten later observations gives first-interval exposure
$$
T_1=120+10\times28=400,
\qquad
\widehat\theta_1=\frac7{400}.
$$
Among the ten observations beyond day 28, eight are deaths, and their exposure beyond 28 is
$$
T_2=2080-10\times28=1800,
\qquad
\widehat\theta_2=\frac8{1800}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Under the two-rate alternative, maximize the <piecewise-exponential survival model> likelihood separately to obtain the estimates in part (i). Under $H_0:\theta_1=\theta_2=\theta$, there are 15 deaths and 2200 days of exposure, so $\widehat\theta=15/2200$. Twice the maximized log-likelihood difference is
$$
2\left[
7\log\frac7{400}+8\log\frac8{1800}
-15\log\frac{15}{2200}
\right].
$$
Under the null this <likelihood-ratio test> statistic is asymptotically $\chi_1^2$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.
